1. INTRODUCTION

Scope is a framework built around an extensible implementation of
the Model-View-Controller (MVC) pattern. It provides an easy-to-use Java
library that can be used as a basis for component-oriented application 
development following the layered architecture detailed in Sun's J2EE
and in Cheesman/Daniels: UML Components.
   
   
2. STATUS

Scope is ready for its v1.0 release.
Javadoc is quite complete, but the documentation still need more work.

A set of samples is provided, covering 
Swing, XML/XSL Servlet and JSP implementations.

The JSP implementation is new, very basic, and
meant only as a starting point for further development.

A separate CVS module "scope-inprogress" houses work
in progress before moving to the stable "scope" module
for general distribution. Developers can feel free to 
add any new functionality into this module, even if it 
doesn't quite work yet.

Supported JDKs are:
  - JDK 1.3: full support
  - JDK 1.4: partial support (should work, but the new swing components 
    in this JDK are not yet supported)

3. DISTRIBUTIONS

Scope is distributed in both source and binary formats as follows:

	3.1 Source distribution (scope-src-1.0.tar.gz)
        The entire build environment for Scope developers.

	3.2 Documentation distribution (scope-doc-1.0.tar.gz)
        Scope website and API javadoc.

	3.3 Swing sample distribution (scope-swingsample-1.0.tar.gz)
        Double-clickable jar (and necessary libraries) for the Swing
        samples.

	3.3 Servlet sample distribution (scope-servletsample-1.0.tar.gz)
        Deployable war (and necessary libraries) for the servlet
        samples (includes Saxon)

	3.4 Debug binary distribution (scope-debug-1.0.tar.gz)
        Scope jar and necessary libraries, assertions active, debug
        symbols included in classes, non-optimized build. For 
        debugging applications using Scope.

	3.5 Runtime binary distribution (scope-bin-1.0.tar.gz)
        Scope jar and necessary libraries, assertions stripped, no debug,
        optimized build. For distribution with applications using Scope.

Note that Scope uses Apache's commons-logging facade. This will use
log4j or jdk1.4 logging if either is available, else logging is thrown away.
commons-logging can also be configured to use Apache's logkit or a
built-in logger that dumps output to System.out. See the documentation
from http://jakarta.apache.org/commons/ for further details.

   
4. SAMPLES

	4.1 Swing examples
	
	The Swing examples can be run individually, or via a "Launcher" application
	that embeds the examples along with their documentation for easy
	browsing.

	The Launcher is the main class that will run by double-clicking on
	the "scope-swingsample" distribution jar.

	4.2 Servlet examples
	
	The Servlet examples can be run by deploying the "scope-servletsample" 
	distribution war and then browsing to (assuming a local Tomcat install):
        http://localhost:8080/scope
		

5. THIRD PARTY LIBRARIES

Scope is dependent on the following third-party libraries, available in the
various Scope distributions:
	commons-logging prerelease <http://jakarta.apache.org>
	jsdk 2.2 <http://www.javasoft.com>

The XML/XSLT servlet implementation requires an XML Parser and 
XSLT Processor. Scope is distributed with Saxon 6.5

The unit tests are dependent on
	junit 3.7 <http://www.junit.org>

The "filefind" sample is dependent on:
	jakarta-oro-2.0 <http://jakarta.apache.org>

